EXPLAINERS['advocacy/explainers/ice-santa-barbara-county'] = {
    'eyebrow': 'In plain words',
    'heading': 'What the data shows about ICE in Santa Barbara County',
    'body': """
## What is happening in Santa Barbara County?
Federal immigration enforcement has become part of daily life across Santa Barbara County. On July 10, 2025, federal agents carried out an operation at a Glass House Farms site in Carpinteria while a larger raid unfolded in Camarillo, just over the county line. The [Santa Barbara County Sheriff's Office said it was told in advance but took no part](https://kioncentralcoast.com/?p=1204131). The graphics above map reported ICE activity from January 1 to July 18, 2026 and count deaths in ICE custody through July 20, 2026. SBWPC's letter, Standing Together for Justice and Human Dignity, honors the lives of Lorenzo Salgado Araujo and Joan Sebastian Duran Guerrero.

## What does SB 54 require?
SB 54, the California Values Act, was signed in late 2017. It [limits how state and local law enforcement can help federal agents detain or arrest immigrants](https://timesofsandiego.com/immigration/2026/04/03/ice-arrests-santa-barbara-county-jails-sb-54/), with exceptions for people who have serious, recent criminal convictions. Under the TRUTH Act, each sheriff must [hold an annual public meeting](https://www.newspress.com/2026/06/22/truth-act-forum-ice-arrests-county-jails/) and share how many people were transferred from jails into immigration custody.

## What do the numbers show?
For 2025, the Sheriff's Office told the state it [transferred 12 people to ICE under SB 54](https://www.newspress.com/2026/04/21/santa-barbara-county-supervisors-vote-to-keep-ice-out-of-county-properties-but-enforcement-questions-remain/). County materials for the 2026 TRUTH Act forum say [ICE asked about or requested access to 221 inmates in 2025](https://www.independent.com/?p=744841). Of those, 193 did not qualify under SB 54 and got no response, and 28 qualified.

That is not the whole picture. A News-Press investigation [found dozens more people arrested in the lobbies and parking lots of county jails](https://www.newspress.com/2026/06/22/truth-act-forum-ice-arrests-county-jails/), many with no conviction listed in ICE records. Attorneys and legal observers who keep their own list [counted at least 13 people with records too minor to qualify for transfer who were arrested at local jails in one year](https://timesofsandiego.com/immigration/2026/04/03/ice-arrests-santa-barbara-county-jails-sb-54/). Sheriff Bill Brown has said his office does not cooperate with ICE beyond what SB 54 allows. He has also said he [will not count or monitor federal arrests made in jail lobbies or parking lots](https://www.newspress.com/?p=13403).

## How have local leaders responded?
On April 21, 2026, the Board of Supervisors [voted 4-1 to bar ICE from using county properties, parks and recreation areas](https://www.newspress.com/2026/04/21/santa-barbara-county-supervisors-vote-to-keep-ice-out-of-county-properties-but-enforcement-questions-remain/) for unauthorized activities, while acknowledging the rule may be hard to enforce. At the June 23 forum, Sheriff Brown said he would rather ICE make arrests inside the jail where state law allows it. [Most public commenters opposed the Sheriff's policies](https://www.kcbx.org/immigration/2026-06-24/santa-barbara-sheriff-on-ice-i-would-much-rather-they-arrest-people-in-our-jail).

## Who holds the power?
SBWPC's second set of graphics, covering the Sheriff and SB 54, asks who holds the power to act: the Sheriff, the Board of Supervisors or ICE. It also shows where an accountability gap remains. SBWPC's published position on immigration is that it opposes local law enforcement collaborating with ICE and the targeting of immigrant communities, and that immigration policy must protect people from intimidation, family separation, inhumane detention and discrimination while ensuring due process and equal protection.

## What can you do?
Read the full [SBWPC positions](/about/positions). Attend the annual TRUTH Act forum and ask for the numbers in writing. Write to your county supervisor. Read how federal pressure on volunteers has grown in our [VC Defensa explainer](/advocacy/explainers/vc-defensa). If you want to help build this work, [join SBWPC](/join).

## Sources
[Santa Barbara News-Press, April 21, 2026](https://www.newspress.com/2026/04/21/santa-barbara-county-supervisors-vote-to-keep-ice-out-of-county-properties-but-enforcement-questions-remain/)
[Santa Barbara News-Press, June 22, 2026](https://www.newspress.com/2026/06/22/truth-act-forum-ice-arrests-county-jails/)
[Times of San Diego, April 3, 2026](https://timesofsandiego.com/immigration/2026/04/03/ice-arrests-santa-barbara-county-jails-sb-54/)
[Santa Barbara Independent, TRUTH Act forum notice](https://www.independent.com/2026/06/11/truth-act-forum-set-for-june-23-2026/)
[KCBX, June 24, 2026](https://www.kcbx.org/immigration/2026-06-24/santa-barbara-sheriff-on-ice-i-would-much-rather-they-arrest-people-in-our-jail)
[KION, July 2025](https://kioncentralcoast.com/?p=1204131)
""",
}

EXPLAINERS['advocacy/explainers/ice-ventura-county'] = {
    'eyebrow': 'In plain words',
    'heading': 'What the law says and what the Ventura County Sheriff does',
    'body': """
## What does SB 54 say about jails?
SB 54, the California Values Act, took effect on January 1, 2018. In plain terms, it says a county jail may not hold someone past their release date just because ICE asked, may not give ICE exclusive office space, and may not allow an ICE interview without the person's written consent. It also leaves one gray area: a sheriff may, but is not required to, notify ICE before releasing someone who meets the law's conviction threshold. SBWPC's graphics point out that Ventura County has chosen to use that option.

## What do the county's own numbers show?
SBWPC compiled these figures from Ventura County Star and Ojai Valley News reporting, which draws on county TRUTH Act reports. In 2024 the Sheriff's Office had 22,076 bookings and received 1,208 ICE detainer requests, the most in eight years and up 51.8 percent from 2022. It sent 133 release notifications to ICE, down from 181 in 2023, and ICE picked up 45 people. Sheriff James Fryhoff has said the office no longer responds to ICE detainers and works with the Public Defender's Office to check SB 54 eligibility before it notifies ICE. He has also said the cooperation that remains is the office's decision under the law's permissive wording.

## What did the Glass House Farms raid change?
On July 10, 2025, federal agents raided Glass House Farms in Camarillo and in Carpinteria across the county line. [Agents used tear gas and other crowd-control devices on demonstrators](https://juliabrownley.house.gov/federal-immigration-agents-blockade-camarillo-farm-clash-with-demonstrators/). The Ventura County Sheriff's Office said its role was traffic control and keeping the peace, and that it [took no part in the federal operation](https://www.yahoo.com/news/undocumented-minors-found-amid-raid-060914999.html). Federal officials said minors were found among those detained. SBWPC's graphics, citing news reports, record 361 arrests, 14 minors, and the death of worker Jaime Alanis Garcia after a fall from a greenhouse roof.

State Senator Henry Stern said that under SB 54 the state [does not and will not cooperate with raids targeting law-abiding immigrants](https://sd27.senate.ca.gov/news/statement-ventura-county-ice-raids-senator-stern).

## Why were records so hard to get?
On November 14, 2025, a [public records lawsuit was filed against the County and the Sheriff](https://firstamendmentcoalition.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2025-11-14-Ventura-Writ-Petition-w-exs.pdf) over records of the July 10 operation. According to SBWPC's timeline, twelve hours of body camera footage was released on March 5, 2026 after a settlement. Public records are how residents check what a law enforcement agency says it did against what it did.

## What did it cost the community?
SBWPC's economic graphic summarizes company filings and academic estimates. Glass House Farms reported quarterly revenue of $38.4 million in the third quarter of 2025, down from $63.8 million a year earlier, and a 2025 net loss of $45.06 million. UC Merced researchers estimate that undocumented workers make up about 60 percent of California's agricultural workforce. When a workplace is raided, the effects reach families, nearby shops and the local economy.

## What does SBWPC say?
SBWPC's published position on immigration opposes local law enforcement collaborating with ICE and the targeting of immigrant communities, and calls for due process and equal protection for everyone. Residents are entitled to see the data, which is why the annual TRUTH Act forum matters. Read how the same pressure has reached volunteers in the [VC Defensa explainer](/advocacy/explainers/vc-defensa), or see the [Santa Barbara County version](/advocacy/explainers/ice-santa-barbara-county).

## Sources
[Ventura County Star, via Rep. Julia Brownley's office, July 10, 2025](https://juliabrownley.house.gov/federal-immigration-agents-blockade-camarillo-farm-clash-with-demonstrators/)
[Los Angeles Times via Yahoo News, July 2025](https://www.yahoo.com/news/undocumented-minors-found-amid-raid-060914999.html)
[Statement from State Senator Henry Stern](https://sd27.senate.ca.gov/news/statement-ventura-county-ice-raids-senator-stern)
[Public records petition, November 14, 2025](https://firstamendmentcoalition.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/2025-11-14-Ventura-Writ-Petition-w-exs.pdf)
""",
}

EXPLAINERS['advocacy/explainers/sable-oil'] = {
    'eyebrow': 'In plain words',
    'heading': 'Sable Offshore and the fight over the Santa Barbara coast',
    'body': """
## What is Sable trying to do?
Sable Offshore bought three offshore platforms, a processing facility and a pipeline system on the Santa Barbara coast in 2024. The company wants to restart oil production and move the oil through the Las Flores pipelines, which run along the Gaviota Coast. The pipelines were shut down after the 2015 Refugio spill. Estimates of how much oil that spill released range from [more than 100,000 gallons, per NOAA](https://www.fisheries.noaa.gov/inport/item/57720/full-list), to about [451,500 gallons, per the Environmental Defense Center](https://www.environmentaldefensecenter.org/?p=4026).

## Why has Sable lost so often in court?
SBWPC's first graphic lists the losses. In November 2024 the California Coastal Commission ordered Sable to stop work. In July 2025 a Santa Barbara Superior Court judge issued a preliminary injunction requiring Sable to confirm all state approvals before restarting. In October 2025 the court upheld the Coastal Commission's authority and an $18 million fine. On December 16, 2025, the Board of Supervisors denied the transfer of permits to Sable.

## What changed in 2026?
In March 2026 the federal government invoked the Defense Production Act to order the pipeline restarted, and Sable began moving oil. On April 17, Judge Donna Geck ruled that the federal order [does not nullify the state injunction](https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/04/17/governor-newsom-exposes-trumps-sable-offshore-pipeline-lie-one-month-of-oil-prices-have-only-gone-up/). The Center for Biological Diversity called it [the first time a court found the order does not relieve Sable of state law](https://www.independent.com/2026/04/17/sable-offshores-federal-defense-falls-flat-in-santa-barbara-court/).

The story did not end there. In August 2026, a federal judge in Los Angeles, Stephen V. Wilson, [ruled that the federal order allows Sable to move oil through its onshore pipeline](https://www.velaw.com/insights/california-pipeline-ruling-signals-expansive-defense-production-act-preemption-power/). The same ruling [found that Sable violated a federal consent decree and fined the company about $1.45 million](https://dailyjournal.com/article/393881-judge-backs-federal-authority-over-sable-pipeline-but-fines-company-1-45m). The state court and the federal court have now reached different conclusions about the same pipeline. Environmental groups and state officials are expected to keep contesting it, and SBWPC's wins-and-losses graphic shows only the state side of the record.

## Who is opposing Sable?
SBWPC's "Wall of Resistance" graphic maps the opposition: courts, state agencies such as the Coastal Commission, the Office of the State Fire Marshal and State Parks, local government including the Board of Supervisors and the District Attorney, the California Attorney General, and groups including the Environmental Defense Center, the Center for Biological Diversity, the Wishtoyo Foundation and Surfrider Foundation. SBWPC's community guide also notes SB 237, a 2025 state law, which sets stricter safety requirements.

## Why does this matter to SBWPC?
SBWPC's published position on the environment supports urgent, science-based climate action, reduced reliance on fossil fuels, and protection of open spaces and wildlife habitat. This fight is also about who decides: a community and its elected leaders, or an emergency order from Washington.

## What can you do?
Use the community guide above to find who to contact: your state and federal representatives and the Attorney General. Follow the legal updates from the groups named in the graphics. Read SBWPC's [positions](/about/positions), and [join SBWPC](/join) to take part in advocacy work.

## Sources
[Office of the Governor, April 17, 2026](https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/04/17/governor-newsom-exposes-trumps-sable-offshore-pipeline-lie-one-month-of-oil-prices-have-only-gone-up/)
[Santa Barbara Independent, April 17, 2026](https://www.independent.com/2026/04/17/sable-offshores-federal-defense-falls-flat-in-santa-barbara-court/)
[Environmental Defense Center, April 17, 2026](https://www.environmentaldefensecenter.org/?p=6483)
[Center for Biological Diversity, April 17, 2026](https://biologicaldiversity.org/w/news/press-releases/judge-sable-in-noncompliance-with-preliminary-injunction-blocking-santa-barbara-oil-pipeline-restart-2026-04-17/)
[Vinson and Elkins, federal ruling analysis](https://www.velaw.com/insights/california-pipeline-ruling-signals-expansive-defense-production-act-preemption-power/)
[Daily Journal, federal ruling](https://dailyjournal.com/article/393881-judge-backs-federal-authority-over-sable-pipeline-but-fines-company-1-45m)
[NOAA, Refugio spill](https://www.fisheries.noaa.gov/inport/item/57720/full-list)
""",
}

EXPLAINERS['advocacy/explainers/vc-defensa'] = {
    'eyebrow': 'In plain words',
    'heading': 'What happened to VC Defensa volunteers, and why it matters',
    'body': """
## What happened on May 13, 2026?
Around 3 a.m. on May 13, 2026, agents from Homeland Security Investigations (HSI) executed search warrants at the homes of volunteers with VC Defensa, an immigrant rights group in Ventura County. VC Defensa says the agents hit four locations and [took phones and computers](https://spectrumlocalnews.com/nc/coastal/politics/2026/05/15/vc-defensa-hsi-raids). No one was arrested. The group's attorney called the raids an intimidation tactic against people exercising their right to organize.

## What does the federal government say?
The Department of Homeland Security said HSI [executed several search warrants on individuals associated with an anti-ICE organization](https://lataco.com/vc-defensa-legal-coalition-restraining-order). SBWPC's graphic, citing federal court records reported by the Ventura County Star, notes that as of May 13 no volunteer had been charged with a crime. Oxnard police confirmed they were told in advance that federal agents would be working in the area but [were given no further details](https://keyt.com/news/ventura-county/2026/05/13/federal-agents-detain-immigrant-rights-group-volunteers-in-ventura-county-wednesday-morning/).

## Is this the first action against the group?
No. After the July 10, 2025 Glass House Farms raid, federal authorities [charged two Ventura County activists with obstructing immigration enforcement](https://spectrumlocalnews.com/ca/california/public-safety/2025/10/30/vc-defensa-ventura-county-ice-), describing it as part of a broader crackdown on groups that interfere with federal law enforcement. L.A. Taco reported that [50 to 60 members, volunteers and activists have faced raids, searches or seizures](https://lataco.com/vc-defensa-legal-coalition-restraining-order). SBWPC's timeline lists a series of actions between July 2025 and May 2026.

## What does VC Defensa do?
VC Defensa says it was founded in 2024 and has organized hundreds of know-your-rights trainings. It runs neighborhood patrols that alert residents to immigration enforcement, offers legal support, and helps families. Federal officials describe members as interfering with officers. The group and its supporters describe lawful observation and community defense. Those two accounts are at the center of the dispute, and a court is the place to settle it.

## Why does it matter beyond Ventura County?
SBWPC's graphic describes the raids as an escalation of federal pressure on community-based immigrant defense networks nationwide. SBWPC's published position on immigration calls for protection from intimidation and for due process and equal protection under the law. Whatever one thinks of the group's tactics, the question for a democracy is whether people can observe, film and organize around government action without being targeted for it. That is a civil rights question for every community, including Santa Barbara County.

## What can you do?
Know your rights. Document encounters from a safe distance. Support legal defense, and connect with your local rapid response network. SBWPC's graphics on this page list organizations and contacts. Read our [Ventura County](/advocacy/explainers/ice-ventura-county) and [Santa Barbara County](/advocacy/explainers/ice-santa-barbara-county) explainers for local context, and [join SBWPC](/join) to push for oversight.

## Sources
[Spectrum News 1, May 15, 2026](https://spectrumlocalnews.com/ca/california/politics/2026/05/15/vc-defensa-hsi-raids)
[KEYT, May 13, 2026](https://keyt.com/news/ventura-county/2026/05/13/federal-agents-detain-immigrant-rights-group-volunteers-in-ventura-county-wednesday-morning/)
[Santa Barbara Independent, May 2026](https://www.independent.com/?p=738765)
[L.A. Taco, VC Defensa legal coalition](https://lataco.com/vc-defensa-legal-coalition-restraining-order)
[Spectrum News 1, October 30, 2025](https://spectrumlocalnews.com/ca/california/public-safety/2025/10/30/vc-defensa-ventura-county-ice-)
[People's Dispatch, May 18, 2026](https://peoplesdispatch.org/2026/05/18/we-are-not-afraid-organizers-in-california-vow-to-continue-community-defense-after-federal-raids/)
""",
}

EXPLAINERS['advocacy/explainers/voting-rights-act'] = {
    'eyebrow': 'In plain words',
    'heading': 'How the Supreme Court narrowed the Voting Rights Act',
    'body': """
## What did the Supreme Court decide?
On April 29, 2026, in Louisiana v. Callais, the Supreme Court ruled 6-3 that [Louisiana's second majority-Black congressional district was an unconstitutional racial gerrymander](https://www.faegredrinker.com/en/insights/publications/2026/4/supreme-court-decides-louisiana-v-callais). The decision also changed what a challenger must prove under Section 2 of the Voting Rights Act (VRA), the provision used for decades to challenge maps and voting rules that dilute minority voting power. Justice Samuel Alito wrote for the majority. Justice Elena Kagan, in dissent, called it the [latest chapter in the majority's "now-completed demolition" of the Voting Rights Act](https://www.acslaw.org/scotus_update/louisiana-v-callais/).

## How did we get here?
SBWPC's domino graphic traces four steps. The VRA was signed in 1965 and used federal oversight, called preclearance, to block discriminatory voting changes in places with a history of discrimination. In 2013, Shelby County v. Holder struck down the formula that decided which places needed preclearance. In 2021, Brnovich v. DNC [made Section 2 challenges to voting rules harder](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/states-of-play/justices-leave-little-of-voting-rights-act-left-after-new-ruling). Callais is the third major narrowing in 13 years.

## What changes in practice?
Before Callais, a plaintiff could win a vote dilution case by showing that a map had discriminatory results. Under the new framework, legal analysts say courts must [focus on intent and separate racial discrimination from partisan goals](https://www.findlaw.com/?p=213981). The NAACP Legal Defense Fund argues the decision [lets partisan aims serve as a defense for discrimination](https://www.naacpldf.org/case-issue/louisiana-v-callais/). Others read the ruling more narrowly: [the majority did not strike down Section 2 itself](https://www.csmonitor.com/layout/set/amphtml/USA/Justice/2026/0429/voting-rights-supreme-court-louisiana-callais) but set a new test for applying it. Both readings agree that Section 2 claims are now harder to win. The Congressional Research Service describes the ruling as [significantly narrowing the circumstances](https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/LSB11431.html) in which a vote dilution challenge can succeed.

## Why does it matter in California?
The decision shapes how every state can draw maps, including how much weight race may receive. SBWPC's second graphic, "Anatomy of Erasure," connects the ruling to wider barriers it tracks, such as voter roll purges, mail ballot rules and drop box closures. Its figures come from groups such as Democracy Docket and the Brennan Center, listed in the graphic. SBWPC works for gender, race and LGBTQ+ equality, and the voting power of Black women and other women of color is part of that work.

## What happens next?
Two routes are in view. Congress could pass new legislation, such as the John Lewis Voting Rights Advancement Act. States can pass their own voting rights acts, as Virginia did in 2021, and state courts may become the main place where these cases are decided.

## What can you do?
Check your registration at the [California Secretary of State's voter status tool](https://voterstatus.sos.ca.gov/). Ask your members of Congress where they stand on restoring the Voting Rights Act. Support the groups named in SBWPC's graphic, and see how SBWPC [endorses candidates](/elections/endorsement-process) who support voting rights. [Join SBWPC](/join) to work on it with us.

## Sources
[Faegre Drinker, Louisiana v. Callais](https://www.faegredrinker.com/en/insights/publications/2026/4/supreme-court-decides-louisiana-v-callais)
[Congressional Research Service, LSB11431](https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/LSB11431.html)
[NAACP Legal Defense Fund](https://www.naacpldf.org/case-issue/louisiana-v-callais/)
[Bloomberg Law, April 29, 2026](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/states-of-play/justices-leave-little-of-voting-rights-act-left-after-new-ruling)
[American Constitution Society](https://www.acslaw.org/scotus_update/louisiana-v-callais/)
[Christian Science Monitor, April 29, 2026](https://www.csmonitor.com/layout/set/amphtml/USA/Justice/2026/0429/voting-rights-supreme-court-louisiana-callais)
[FindLaw](https://www.findlaw.com/?p=213981)
""",
}

EXPLAINERS['advocacy/explainers/camino-dolores-huerta'] = {
    'eyebrow': 'In plain words',
    'heading': 'Why Santa Barbara should consider Camino Dolores Huerta',
    'body': """
## What is the proposal?
Santa Barbara is weighing a new name for Calle Cesar Chavez, a street on the Lower Eastside. SBWPC supports renaming it Camino Dolores Huerta. The street began as Salsipuedes Street, a name that loosely means "get out if you can," and [became Calle Cesar Chavez on September 13, 1997](https://953thebeach.com/?p=222769).

## Why is the name being reconsidered now?
In March 2026, Dolores Huerta [said publicly that Cesar Chavez had sexually assaulted her on two occasions](https://www.newspress.com/?p=6790). Santa Barbara City Councilmember Oscar Gutierrez then asked whether the street should be renamed. On March 31, the City Council [directed the City Administrator to begin the process](https://santabarbaraca.gov/news/meeting-recap-renaming-calle-cesar-chavez).

## Where does the process stand?
At a May 11, 2026 meeting, the Neighborhood Advisory Council recommended Calle Si Se Puede as its primary name, with two alternatives for equal consideration: Calle Dolores Huerta, and a name recommended by the Coastal Band of the Chumash Nation. The City states that this [action does not finalize a name change](https://santabarbaraca.gov/news/meeting-recap-renaming-calle-cesar-chavez). SBWPC's graphics note that 86 businesses and property addresses would be affected by any rename, which is why the city is gathering community input before the Council decides. Check the City's page for the current status.

## Why Dolores Huerta?
Huerta co-founded the United Farm Workers with Chavez in 1962. She was a lead negotiator on the 1970 grape contracts, helped win the California Agricultural Labor Relations Act of 1975, the first state law to give farmworkers the right to bargain collectively, and received the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2012. A Santa Barbara Independent essay by Teri Jory makes the local point: Huerta co-founded the union, yet [Chavez was the only one of the two honored with a Santa Barbara street name](https://www.independent.com/2026/05/17/renaming-calle-cesar-chavez-with-purpose/).

## What does the gender gap in street names look like?
SBWPC's graphics, citing historian Neal Graffy, show that the 52 original streets laid out in 1851 honor almost no women. They also cite a European Data Journalism Network finding that, on average, about 91 percent of streets named after individuals honor men. SBWPC's reasoning is that public names teach residents who matters. Seeing women leaders on street signs matters for girls and for the historical record.

## What are the other views?
Residents and officials have offered other ideas, including restoring the Salsipuedes name, honoring Huerta with a plaza or monument instead, choosing a name from the Chumash community, or naming the street for a local farmworker. Some residents worry about cost and disruption to businesses. SBWPC encourages everyone affected to take part.

## What can you do?
Submit comments to the City of Santa Barbara and watch for Council and Neighborhood Advisory Council agendas. Read the [Independent essay](https://www.independent.com/2026/05/17/renaming-calle-cesar-chavez-with-purpose/) and the City's [meeting recap](https://santabarbaraca.gov/news/meeting-recap-renaming-calle-cesar-chavez). [Join SBWPC](/join) to help advocate for equity in public space.

## Sources
[City of Santa Barbara, meeting recap](https://santabarbaraca.gov/news/meeting-recap-renaming-calle-cesar-chavez)
[Santa Barbara Independent, May 17, 2026](https://www.independent.com/2026/05/17/renaming-calle-cesar-chavez-with-purpose/)
[Santa Barbara News-Press, April 8, 2026](https://www.newspress.com/2026/04/08/santa-barbara-takes-first-steps-toward-renaming-calle-cesar-chavez)
[Noozhawk, Neighborhood Advisory Council discussion](https://www.noozhawk.com/?p=813210)
[Edhat, NAC options](https://www.edhat.com/news/calle-cesar-chavez-rename-santa-barbara/)
[95.3 The Beach, March 23, 2026](https://953thebeach.com/?p=222769)
""",
}

